Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the tally of death and destruction keeps  growing in the northern california  wildfires  thick clouds of ash are turning day into  night  2020 has been another record-setting  wildfire season  over the last few years wildfires have  been ranging not just in the western us  but all around the world fires in the  amazon rainforest and australia's brush  fires  are so massive that they can be seen  from space  and while fires have long been a natural  part of many forest ecosystems  higher temperatures and longer droughts  caused by climate change  are drying out forests and turning trees  into tinder  meaning that in areas like california  wildfire seasons  have become supercharged towns like  paradise california  where the 2018 campfire destroyed over  80 percent of its homes  are now grappling with how to adapt to  this new age of fire  starting from essentially the ground up  it is literally like the surface of the  moon  there two years later as a town still  works to rebuild  the local parks and recreation district  is beginning an ambitious plan  to not just restore paradise but make  sure it can better defend itself  from the next fire  city lab reporter laura bliss went to  see this first  hand so laura you just went to paradise  can you sort of explain what's it like  to be there now  it was a pretty haunting experience just  thousands and thousands of homes  literally reduced to shells kind of  gutted out foundations  it really still is this landscape of  disaster  [Music]  so i guess what what has the skill of  rebuilding been like i checked with the  city and there's been about 400 homes  that have already been reconstructed  and about 1200 that are currently  permitted  even as this kind of instinct to rebuild  is sweeping  over the town and over former residents  there are some people who are thinking a  little bit differently about what the  future of paradise  should look like one resident i spoke to  has actually gone forward with selling  her land so that  eventually it might become part of a  large green belt  encircling the city to potentially  protect it from future wildfires  and the person who's leading this  project is not a member  of city government he's actually the  manager of the paradise recreation and  parks district  my name is dan f sepp i'm the district  manager for the paradise recreation and  park district  so we provided everything from swimming  lessons to  people's first sports experience to  hosting a variety of clubs  and we've been a kind of fabric of the  community for decades  there were quite a few lessons from the  campfire for us one of them was the the  role we potentially have as a parks  district  and dan believes what the park district  can start to do is improve what's known  as the wildland urban  interface it's that line between  kind of a full-blown nature and kind of  wildland areas  and then the more developed space in  paradise that  line was very much blurred or a razor's  edge  so the idea here is that we would take  that very narrow razor sharp  wildland urban interface now and expand  expanded out in such a way that  we would have a buffer at the same time  providing these kind of extraordinary  recreational resources and opportunities  dan and the parks and recreation  district are in the very early phases of  acquiring land  in hopes of connecting them and what  would be basically a giant moat of green  space  so if the nascent plan is fully realized  thousands of acres of land  could be used for respite and play by  the residents of paradise  parks and baseball fields could also  double as future fuel breaks  for fire so areas that could be parks  they could be returned in some cases to  orchards  we're interested in ag tourism in the  area so we're trying to do is take this  idea of defensible space  around a house and expand that to the  ventile space around the community  this plant is taking shape now as  california  and all of the western u.s is on fire  already this year millions of acres have  burned  tens of thousands of people have had to  evacuate with climate change driving up  temperatures and drying out forests  there's no question that the risk of  catastrophe recurring  in communities like paradise and  paradise itself  is very very real but for dan he's  hoping that this plan can make paradise  not just a dire warning of the damage  wildfires can cause  but rather a model on how to better  protect communities in fire prone  regions  earthquakes we have major cities all up  and down the west coast  that sit in major earthquake faults  we've mitigated that  we've engineered our way into having  less danger associated with earthquakes  in major cities  and we can do the same with fire we just  haven't done it yet  this is crystal cold she's a geographer  by training  i call myself a pyro geographer because  almost all of my work over the last 15  20 years  has been across this really broad  spectrum of understanding  how humans and the environment  are affected by and interact with fire  there are a lot of different factors  that contribute to changing wildfires  but climate change is the thing that  amplifies  all of those changes so when we have a  high temperature there's a lot more heat  and that heat translates into fires  being able to burn more easily and  because  they're able to burn more easily the  conditions are drier they're able to  burn a lot hotter  and a lot faster and they're much much  more difficult for firefighters to  contain while around the country the  number of wildland fires have actually  been going down since 1990  the acres burned has at times doubled  the idea in paradise is to use what's  called a green belt to help slow the  destructiveness of future fires  green belts are a type of fuel break  right  uh green belts are of course these areas  where a lot of the understory  is removed under trees that they become  essentially  park-like grasslands this is one of the  jewels of our area this is paradise lake  this uh was a location where the fire  slowed down and stopped on its own you  can definitely see  evidence of fire through this area but  the flame lengths are relatively low on  these trees  look at all of these small trees that  were cut  these are cut before the fire so that  created a more open  understory which carried the fire less  than it would have otherwise  dan wants to use the land that they  require to create more areas that look  like this  we are going to pursue a strategy here  to these oaks that are sprouting up  make them less bushy make it less of a  brush field  over time and encourage one particular  leader out of here to grow into a tree  it's really important to note that it's  not going to be as simple as just  planting some trees and grass and hoping  for the best  fire breaks are not invulnerable to the  spread of fires  high winds especially the ones we've  seen igniting some of the fires of these  catastrophic events in the last couple  of years can carry burning embers as far  as a mile  a common misconception that people have  is that a fire break should just stop a  fire  right that's that's the definition right  it's meant to break a fire's forward  progress and it should do it all by  itself  and that's really a misconception  because that is not what the goal  of a fire break usually is it is  specifically  meant to be a safe place for  firefighters to take a stand  and try and modify fires forward  progress  as it's coming into a community  you know i keep thinking about this with  the story that pulling off this green  belt  feels like a lot for a parks and rec  district to pull off  how much does something like this cost i  think dan's back of the envelope  calculation  was that this is going to require you  know upwards of 10 million dollars  and that's not even including you know  what it's going to take to maintain all  these lands  so just to give you a sense of what this  would entail one of the sections that  dan is targeting for this green buffer  idea  is a stretch of land that runs parallel  to the feather river canyon  it's about seven miles long and about  four thousand acres in total  containing hundreds of properties about  80 percent of that area is privately  owned  so on top of the costs of acquiring the  land it's also just the time  and kind of resources that a little  parks district  may be challenged to work up to turning  these former homes  into parkland also hits on the ultra  sensitive question of plan relocation  which is this idea of government  basically coming in to  assist whole communities of people move  to an area that's safer from a climate  change perspective  and that's a really difficult question  in a community that's already been  deeply traumatized by this  catastrophic experience  you know we're absolutely of the mind we  have to be respectful of our  decisions by landowners i think the idea  is right now there's such a receptivity  to people wanting to do  some things that help the community even  if they choose to rebuild  then maybe they will provide an easement  or a permission to  have that trail that goes to the bottom  of their property that allows for fuels  reduction  protects their property in exchange for  a little bit of recreational value  i think that idea is pretty persuasive  for a lot of people in our community  another issue dan faces is that while  there is a lot of anecdotal evidence  that green belts can help  there actually isn't that much  scientific evidence that confirms their  effectiveness  one conservationist i talked to for the  story referred to green buffers is this  funny combination of common sense with  not  a lot of scientific backup but experts  say that the signs are definitely out  there that they can work  it's something that is you know not  really making it into the scientific  literature because it's been relatively  rare  we don't have the sort of rigorous  reproducible scientific process  that is often favored by scientific  journals  but the anecdotal data are very much  supportive of this i actually published  a study  last year about a series of fire breaks  and what was  essentially a green belt around the  community of montecito california  between the years of 1999 and 2017  montecito would spend over 1.6 million  dollars  on wildfire vulnerability reduction  activities  and over years they did it little by  little it's individual properties  it's along roads where they have space  to work  and when we actually went back and  looked at the satellite data  it looks a little bit like a green  necklace and in 2017  the thomas fire would put that work to  the test  the calmer conditions in santa barbara  county wednesday didn't mean  flames stopped threatening multi-million  dollar homes in montecito  this is the fifth largest fire in  california history  and when that fire hit montecito there  were winds blowing  over 60 miles an hour there were gusts  up to 72 miles an hour  and that was the last thing we had  recorded before the weather station  burned down  and when it got to that green belt  along the edge of the town it basically  did exactly what  the green belt was designed to do which  is it died down  it allowed firefighters to move in close  and work safely to put water on the edge  of the fire and actually  drop it down to the ground and put it  out  and it is one of the few cases that is  really well documented retrospectively  and spatially of a greenbelt working  well we're trying to find more of those  but  but it's a challenge  what we're talking about is an idea that  how do we live with fire in this  landscape potentially  we can serve a model on a whole bunch of  different levels  not just the parks and how to protect  the community but how do we take care of  our people  we can do all the science we want but if  people don't see how it applies to them  and how it can translate into an action  that they can actually take  then the science doesn't actually you  know become applied in any way shape or  form and we don't change  actions on the ground there's been a lot  of interest  in how we protect paradise there's  lessons here  we're far enough along that we can  demonstrate some of those lessons to  those unfortunate communities that have  suffered very similar situations  but also there's models in here that  that are going to  you know help people live better better  lives in these mountain communities  [Music]

